    "summary": "High‑energy second set with stellar Morning Dew and seamless jams",
    "blurb": "The November 1 1973 show shines on its second set, delivering a scorching Morning Dew that rolls straight into an inspired Playing In The Band sandwich. Uncle John's Band, He's Gone, Wharf Rat and Mississippi Half‑Step all sparkle, backed by a prominent Phil Lesh bass line. Soundboard recordings capture the performance with crystal clarity.",
    "review": "The Dead’s McGaw Memorial Hall concert on November 1 1973 is a textbook example of the Wall‑of‑Sound era at its finest. While the first set is solid, it’s the second half that truly elevates the night: an unforgettable Morning Dew launches into a seamless Playing In The Band > Uncle John's Band > Playing In The Band sequence, each transition feeling effortless and purposeful. From there the band rides a jazzy Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo into a thunderous He's Gone, then rolls through Truckin' and caps it off with a powerful Wharf Rat that showcases Garcia’s tight lead work and strong vocal delivery. Phil Lesh dominates the mix in true “Phil Zone” fashion, his bass cutting cleanly through the wall of guitars while Bill Kreutzmann adds a supple, jazzy groove on the Half‑Step. The recordings capture every nuance – from Keith Godchaux’s subtle keyboards to Donna Jean’s backing vocals – with an almost clinical clarity that lets listeners hear the full depth of the band’s interplay.",
